# Skill: Fix -Werror=format and format string mismatches in C/C++

## Description
This error occurs when the format specifier in a printf-like function (e.g., printf, fprintf, sprintf, dlt_vlog, etc.) does not match the type of the argument provided. When compiled with -Werror=format, this warning becomes a build-breaking error.

### Example Error
```
error: format '%u' expects argument of type 'unsigned int', but argument 4 has type 'int32_t' {aka 'int'} [-Werror=format=]
```

## How to fix
1. Identify the format specifier and the argument type in the error message.
2. Change the format specifier to match the argument type:
   - Use %d for int, int32_t
   - Use %u for unsigned int, uint32_t
   - Use %ld for long, %lu for unsigned long
   - Use %lld for long long, %llu for unsigned long long
   - Use %f for float/double
   - For fixed-width types (int32_t, uint32_t, etc.), you can use macros from <inttypes.h> (e.g., PRId32, PRIu32)
3. Rebuild and verify the warning/error is gone.

## Example Fix
// Before:
printf("Value=%u\n", my_int32);
// After:
printf("Value=%d\n", my_int32);

// Or, for portable code:
printf("Value=%" PRId32 "\n", my_int32);

// Mixed signed/unsigned arguments:
// Before:
printf("A=%d, B=%d, C=%d\n", a_unsigned, b_unsigned, c_signed);
// After:
printf("A=%u, B=%u, C=%d\n", a_unsigned, b_unsigned, c_signed);

// Wrong: using %u for int32_t (signed int)
// Before:
printf("Buffer size = %u\n", my_int32);
// After:
printf("Buffer size = %d\n", my_int32);

// Wrong: using %u for int32_t (signed int) in memory allocation log
// Before:
printf("Cannot allocate memory for payload buffer of size %u!\n", my_int32);
// After:
printf("Cannot allocate memory for payload buffer of size %d!\n", my_int32);

// Wrong: using %d for unsigned int (enum/bitfield/unsigned)
// Before:
printf("Wrong parameter for mode: %d\n", my_unsigned);
// After:
printf("Wrong parameter for mode: %u\n", my_unsigned);

## References
- GCC documentation: https://gcc.gnu.org/onlinedocs/gcc/Warning-Options.html#index-Wformat
- C99 format macros: https://en.cppreference.com/w/c/types/integer
